## 5. Color and Mood

Locked Aether palette (black ground, semantic colour rationed hard). **Mood: a calm supervisory control room** — the composure of someone who can see, at a glance, which loop a problem belongs to. Reads as a plant-control overview, not a motivational infographic.

Colour logic (kept strict so the four loops read as an *instrument*, not a four-colour chart):

1. **Structure / the four loops / the bus / all labels = cool slate, ruler-drawn.** The loops are **monochrome** on purpose — differentiated by *label, clock-badge, and position*, never by decorative hue. This is what keeps it an engineering schematic.
2. **The SHIELD veto bus = the dominant authoritative weight** — heaviest line, the "master" treatment — so it crowns and caps the panel structurally (authority by line-weight and position, not by a loud colour).
3. **Green (rationed) = "in range / loop holding / progress live"** — the clean lamp on each gauge and the green stretch of the body-fat gate (8–12% push band). Green means *the system holds*.
4. **Amber = every trigger-fired / caution / pull-back state** — stall, getting soft, ceiling-near, tier step-down, "keep some E2 / don't escalate vertically," the shield's limit warnings.
5. **Red — EXACTLY ONE object:** the vertical-escalation misroute (the rejected "stall → +test" wire at the food-first gate). Red appears nowhere else.

Background dark/graphite; the closed-loop return arrows drawn so the *circulation* of each loop is legible (it must look alive/turning, not static). Clock-rate badges in neutral mono so "four different clocks" reads through type, not through four tints.

